Q: Python - How to write a Windows path to a json file? A: <h3>In response to your edit:</h3>
<p>JSON is not a format you can just store plain text with no type in. You cannot do this:</p>
<pre><code>{
  &quot;message&quot;: This is a standard sentence of plain text.
}
</code></pre>
<p>But you can do this:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;message&quot;: &quot;This is a standard sentence of plain text.&quot;
}
</code></pre>
<p>and wherever you extract the value from, universally, in whatever language, just format it.</p>

Q: Does HikariCP supports command timeout in Spring Boot application similar to C# A: <p>There is no such setting in HikariCP as HikariCP is just a connection pool, thats responsibility is purely to manage connections in the pool and give them to the requestors.</p>
<p>But you can use <code>@Transactional</code> attribute <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/docs/current/javadoc-api/org/springframework/transaction/annotation/Transactional.html#timeout()" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>timeout</code></a> to do what you described. The value you set here will basically be passed to <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/javase/8/docs/api/java/sql/Statement.html#setQueryTimeout-int-" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>java.sql.Statement.setQueryTimeout</code></a> under the hood (if a single transaction executes several statements, then each statement will use a smaller timeout - the remaining of the total timeout set for the current transaction, e.g. if transaction timeout is 40s and first statement took 5s, the second statement will use 35s timeout) and if timeout is reached, an exception will be thrown.</p>
<p>So, your code will look like this:</p>
<pre><code>@Transactional(timeout = 40)
public void doSomethingHorribleLong() {
  // Use heavy JDBC requests here..
}
</code></pre>
<p>It is also possible to set the default value globally if you use Spring Boot, using <a href="https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/appendix/application-properties/index.html#application-properties.transaction.spring.transaction.default-timeout" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>spring.transaction.default-timeout</code></a> property in your <code>application.properties</code> or <code>application.yml</code>.</p>
<p>The default timeout is <code>-1</code> (infinity).</p>
